---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/pages/PageHead.astro';
import PageFoot from '../components/pages/PageFoot.astro';

const docs = [
  { href: '/docs/getting-started/', label: 'Getting started' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/ports-and-env/', label: 'Ports and environment' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/infra/', label: 'Infra: containers and a database per lane' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/lifecycle/', label: 'Lane lifecycle, throttling and freezing' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/https/', label: 'Hostnames and HTTPS' },
  { href: '/docs/reference/config/', label: 'Config reference' }
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Lanes"
  description="A lane is a running copy of your dev stack with its own ports, hostnames and data. devlanes gives every Git worktree one, from a single committed devlanes.yaml."
  path="/lanes/"
  current="lanes"
>
  <PageHead title="Every worktree gets a lane: its own ports, hostnames and data.">
    <p>
      Lane 0 follows the primary branch. Any other worktree becomes a lane the first time devlanes
      runs in it, with the next index, a block of ports and a domain such as{' '}
      <code>myapp1.localhost</code>. One committed file describes the stack for all of them.
    </p>
  </PageHead>

  <Section id="ports" title="A block of ports and a name for each worktree." stacked>
    <p>
      Each lane gets ten consecutive ports from one machine-wide pool: 20000 to 20009 for main,
      20010 to 20019 for the checkout branch. Every service has the same offset in every lane and
      reads its port from <code>PORT</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lane is part of the hostname, <code>api.myapp1.localhost</code>, so each worktree is its
      own site for cookies and storage. A lane that runs nothing keeps its ports and its data; lane
      2 below is idle.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="devlanes status">
      <pre class="terminal-content terminal-scroll"><span class="command">$ devlanes status</span>
<span class="url">myapp</span>
  <span class="dim">LANE  BRANCH         STATE    SERVICES  CPU    MEM        URL</span>
  0     main           <span class="success">running</span>  3/3       9.0%   286.0 MiB  https://myapp.localhost
  1     feat/checkout  <span class="success">running</span>  3/3       12.6%  341.0 MiB  https://myapp1.localhost
  2     fix/login      <span class="dim">idle     –         –      –</span>          https://myapp2.localhost
  3     agent/perf     <span class="success">running</span>  3/3       38.4%  513.0 MiB  https://myapp3.localhost</pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="config" title="One committed file describes the stack." reverse>
    <p>
      <code>devlanes.yaml</code> sits at the root of the repository and is committed, so every
      worktree has it. <code>lane.domain</code> is <code>myapp.localhost</code> in lane 0 and{' '}
      <code>myapp1.localhost</code> in lane 1, so each web app talks to its own API.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your service only has to listen on <code>$PORT</code>; presets for Vite, Next, Wrangler and
      others pass the right flags for you. Changes meant for your machine alone go in{' '}
      <code>devlanes.local.yaml</code>, which stays out of Git.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="devlanes.yaml">
      <pre class="terminal-content terminal-scroll"><span class="key">services</span>:
  <span class="key">api</span>:
    <span class="key">run</span>: pnpm --filter api dev
    <span class="key">hosts</span>: <span class="string">"api.<span class="expr">${"{{ lane.domain }}"}</span>"</span>

  <span class="key">web</span>:
    <span class="key">preset</span>: vite
    <span class="key">run_script</span>:
      <span class="key">file</span>: web/package.json
      <span class="key">name</span>: dev
    <span class="key">env</span>:
      <span class="key">VITE_API_URL</span>: <span class="string">"<span class="expr">${"{{ services.api.public_url }}"}</span>"</span>
    <span class="key">hosts</span>: <span class="string">"<span class="expr">${"{{ lane.domain }}"}</span>"</span></pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="database" title="Shared infra, a database per lane." stacked>
    <p>
      Containers under <code>infra</code> come from the Compose file you already have and run once
      for every lane, so ten worktrees do not mean ten Postgres servers. A setup step creates a
      database per lane inside it, <code>myapp_main</code> for lane 0 and <code>myapp_lane1</code>{' '}
      for lane 1, and an expression puts its name in <code>DATABASE_URL</code>. A migration in the
      checkout branch leaves main's data alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      One command tears a lane down: its database is dropped and its ports freed for the next
      branch. A branch that needs a container of its own gets one with <code>per_lane</code>.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="devlanes.yaml, continued">
      <pre class="terminal-content terminal-scroll"><span class="key">global_env</span>:
  <span class="key">DATABASE_URL</span>: postgres://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:<span class="expr">${"{{ infra.postgres.port }}"}</span>/<span class="expr">${"{{ lane.db_name }}"}</span>

<span class="key">lanes</span>:
  <span class="key">vars</span>:
    <span class="key">db_name</span>: <span class="string">"myapp_<span class="expr">${"{{ lane.name }}"}</span>"</span>

<span class="key">infra</span>:
  <span class="key">compose</span>: docker-compose.yml
  <span class="key">setup</span>:
    <span class="key">database</span>: |
      <span class="command">docker compose exec -T postgres createdb -U postgres <span class="expr">${"{{ lane.db_name }}"}</span></span>
  <span class="key">teardown</span>:
    <span class="key">database</span>: |
      <span class="command">docker compose exec -T postgres dropdb -U postgres --if-exists --force <span class="expr">${"{{ lane.db_name }}"}</span></span></pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="reload" title="Edit the file, then reload only what changed.">
    <p>
      An edit to <code>devlanes.yaml</code> never touches running services. devlanes shows which
      ones differ from the file, and a reload restarts only those.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="~/dev/myapp-checkout">
      <pre class="terminal-content"><span class="command">$ devlanes reload --no-ui</span>
myapp@1 · feat/checkout
<span class="success">✓</span> services  1 of 1 ready
  <span class="success">✓</span>  api  <span class="url">https://api.myapp1.localhost</span></pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="freeze" title="Idle lanes are frozen, so ten of them cost no CPU." stacked>
    <p>
      Put agents on ten branches at once and walk away. A lane with no requests, connections or
      commands for 15 minutes is suspended with the operating system's own mechanism: its ports,
      data and memory stay, its processes stop using CPU, and the next request wakes it. Overnight,
      the lanes you are not looking at are idle for the machine.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="devlanes status, the next morning">
      <pre class="terminal-content terminal-scroll"><span class="command">$ devlanes status</span>
<span class="url">myapp</span>
  <span class="dim">LANE  BRANCH         STATE              SERVICES  CPU   MEM        URL</span>
  0     main           <span class="success">running</span>            3/3       9.0%  286.0 MiB  https://myapp.localhost
  1     feat/checkout  <span class="success">running</span> <span class="frozen">frozen 7h</span>  3/3       0.0%  341.0 MiB  https://myapp1.localhost
  3     agent/perf     <span class="success">running</span> <span class="frozen">frozen 6h</span>  3/3       0.0%  513.0 MiB  https://myapp3.localhost
  4     agent/search   <span class="success">running</span> <span class="frozen">frozen 8h</span>  3/3       0.0%  402.0 MiB  https://myapp4.localhost</pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="https" title="HTTPS on every lane, nothing to set up." reverse>
    <p>
      Every hostname is served over HTTPS with a certificate from a CA devlanes creates for you and
      asks you to trust once. Browsers resolve <code>*.localhost</code> to your machine on their
      own, so secure cookies, service workers and OAuth callbacks behave on a lane as they do in
      production. Services get the CA too, so one lane service calling another over HTTPS needs no
      workaround. When something is off, one command checks the proxy and the certificates and says
      what to fix.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="devlanes doctor">
      <pre class="terminal-content"><span class="command">$ devlanes doctor</span>
proxy
  <span class="success">✓</span> http on port 80
  <span class="success">✓</span> https on port 443
tls
  <span class="success">✓</span> CA at /home/you/.devlanes/ca/ca.pem
  <span class="success">✓</span> CA trusted by system</pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <PageFoot links={docs} />
</SiteLayout>
